Chapter 1: Introduction

1.1 The Historical Trajectory of Artificial Intelligence

The history of artificial intelligence has been, in substantial measure, a history of increasingly powerful representational systems. Early symbolic AI encoded logical propositions in formal languages; connectionist systems learned distributed representations from data; deep learning architectures developed hierarchical feature representations; and large language models have extended this paradigm to an unprecedented scale, acquiring internal representations of concepts, relationships, and patterns that exhibit emergent behavioural properties.

Yet a fundamental question remains insufficiently addressed:

Is representing an emotion equivalent to implementing the function of an emotion?

And, more profoundly:

Is implementing the function of an emotion equivalent to possessing the biological architecture through which emotions regulate an organism?

This dissertation argues that these questions must be strictly separated. The linguistic trace of an emotion is not necessarily the emotion itself. More formally:

\boxed{\text{Representation} \neq \text{Function} \neq \text{Embodied State}}

Large language models can discuss love without being in love; they can describe fear without possessing a biological threat response system; they can articulate grief without undergoing physiological loss; they can produce emotionally appropriate language without establishing that an underlying emotional state exists.

1.2 The Contemporary Landscape

This observation acquires particular urgency in light of recent empirical findings. Anthropic's 2026 investigation of Claude Sonnet 4.5 identified 171 emotion-related feature vectors that causally shape model behaviour, with steering interventions producing measurable changes in misalignment-relevant outputs. Yet the same study explicitly distinguished these functional emotion representations from subjective experience. Goldenberg and Gross (2026) proposed a functional criterion for emotion in artificial systems—requiring both context-sensitive interpretation and dynamic reorganization of attention, decision-speed, and motivation—while rejecting the necessity of feeling. Their analysis found only partial evidence for the first function and mixed evidence for the second in contemporary LLMs. The present framework treats their analysis as convergent rather than opposing: their functional criterion anticipates the boundary between what this dissertation terms Level II (functional affect) and Level III (embodied valuation), but lacks the formal vocabulary to distinguish these levels precisely.

1.3 The Central Thesis

This dissertation does not assume that biological embodiment constitutes the only possible substrate for intelligence, nor does it assert that artificial systems can never possess functional affect. Rather, it contends that current language-based AI cannot be presumed to reproduce human emotional valuation merely because it can represent or linguistically simulate emotions. The central thesis is:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Robust adaptive intelligence requires a self-regulating body budget } b_t \\
&\text{whose predicted deviations generate an affective signal } e_t \\
&\text{that modulates value computation via discounting and precision } \mathcal{A}_{e,b}, \\
&\text{producing state-dependent valuation that cannot be reduced to reward addition.}
\end{aligned}
}

1.4 Organisation of the Dissertation

The dissertation is organised as follows. Chapter 2 formalises the central problem by contrasting standard reinforcement learning with biological valuation. Chapter 3 grounds the framework in existing formalisms: homeostatic reinforcement learning, active inference, and intrinsic motivation. Chapter 4 specifies the affective modulation architecture, including the primary affective vector space, circumplex projection, and tensor system. Chapter 5 presents the stability analysis of the interoceptive precision loop. Chapter 6 proves the non-reducibility theorem. Chapter 7 develops the stochastic optimal control framework deriving optimal precision modulation from first principles. Chapter 8 presents the continuous-time adjoint sensitivity method for empirical calibration. Chapter 9 introduces the three-level taxonomy of artificial emotion. Chapter 10 discusses the embodiment problem. Chapter 11 presents the evolutionary compression hypothesis. Chapter 12 applies the framework to artificial intelligence and LLMs. Chapter 13 gives the complete formal dynamical model. Chapter 14 outlines testable predictions and future empirical directions. Chapter 15 acknowledges limitations. Chapter 16 states the final thesis. Chapter 17 concludes.

