Chapter 2: The Central Problem

2.1 Standard Reinforcement Learning

Traditional reinforcement learning provides a powerful formalism for intelligent behaviour. A canonical agent maximizes expected discounted cumulative reward:

V^\pi(s) = \mathbb{E}_\pi \left[ \sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \gamma^t r_t \,\middle|\, s_0 = s \right],

where $s_t$ denotes state at time $t$, $r_t$ denotes scalar reward, $\gamma \in [0,1)$ denotes discount factor, and $\pi$ denotes the policy.

This framework has generated substantial progress. However, biological organisms do not appear to operate solely through externally specified scalar rewards. Their decisions are modulated by:

· Physiological state (hunger, thirst, fatigue)
· Arousal and stress responses
· Fear and anxiety
· Attachment and social bonding
· Social status and reputation
· Uncertainty and ambiguity
· Memory and anticipated outcomes
· Internal energy availability
· Interoceptive signals
· Developmental history

Consequently, the value assigned to an external state can change dramatically depending upon the internal state of the organism. A glass of water does not possess a single universal biological value:

V(\text{water} \mid \text{dehydrated}) \gg V(\text{water} \mid \text{hydrated}).

A potential threat does not possess a fixed value either; its significance depends upon physiological state, prior experience, perceived escape probability, social context, and numerous other variables.

2.2 The Biological Value Function

The standard formulation $V(s)$ may therefore be insufficient to characterize biological valuation. A more adequate representation is:

V_B = V_B(s, b, e, m, c),

where:

· $b$ is the bodily/interoceptive state
· $e$ is the affective state
· $m$ is memory and developmental history
· $c$ is contextual and social parameters

This richer formulation constitutes the foundation of the proposed framework. However, the introduction of additional state variables is not, by itself, a theoretical contribution; any reinforcement learning system can incorporate internal variables into its state representation. The critical question is whether the structural relationship between these variables—and specifically the role of affect in modulating value computation—differs from generic state-dependent valuation.

This dissertation argues that it does, and specifies precisely how.

2.3 The Biological Valuation Hypothesis

Biological Valuation Hypothesis. Biological intelligence derives part of its robustness and adaptive flexibility from an evolution-shaped valuation architecture in which bodily and affective states dynamically modulate the interpretation, valuation, learning, and control of environmental states.

Schematically:

s_t + b_t + e_t + m_t + c_t \rightarrow V_t \rightarrow a_t \rightarrow (s_{t+1}, b_{t+1}, e_{t+1}).

This differs fundamentally from a purely feed-forward interpretation of intelligence. The organism is not simply predicting the world; it is continuously regulating its relationship with the world. The valuation process is dynamic, recursive, and embodied.

This hypothesis aligns with Sutskever's (2025) argument that human emotions may serve as a robust biological value function. The present dissertation gives that intuition mathematical content, while acknowledging that Sutskever's broader claim concerns the relationship between emotion and generalization—a question this framework addresses indirectly through its analysis of valuation dynamics but does not fully resolve.

