Chapter 7: Stochastic Optimal Control Framework for Interoceptive Regulation

To derive the Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman (HJB) partial differential equation governing optimal interoceptive precision modulation, we formulate the problem within the framework of continuous-time stochastic optimal control.

7.1 State Dynamics and Cost Functional

Let the system state vector be $\mathbf{x}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} \delta(t) \\ \tilde{\Pi}(t) \end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{d+1}$, where:

· $\delta(t) \in \mathbb{R}^d$ represents the interoceptive prediction error (innovation state)
· $\tilde{\Pi}(t) \in [0, \Delta\Pi_{\max}]$ represents the precision shift relative to baseline $\Pi_0$
· $u(t) \in \mathbb{R}$ represents the active control effort (metabolic/neural drive modulating precision acceleration)

The stochastic differential equations (SDEs) governing state transitions are:

d\delta(t) = -k_r (\Pi_0 + \tilde{\Pi}(t)) \delta(t) \, dt + \sigma_\delta \Vert\delta(t)\Vert \mathbf{I}_d \, dW_1(t)

d\tilde{\Pi}(t) = \left( u(t) - \mu \tilde{\Pi}(t) \right) dt + \sigma_\Pi \tilde{\Pi}(t) \, dW_2(t)

where $dW_1(t) \in \mathbb{R}^d$ and $dW_2(t) \in \mathbb{R}$ are independent standard Wiener processes.

Compact Vector SDE Representation

\mathbf{x}(t) = \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}(t), u(t)) \, dt + \mathbf{G}(\mathbf{x}(t)) \, d\mathbf{W}(t)

where:

\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}, u) = \begin{bmatrix} -k_r (\Pi_0 + \tilde{\Pi}) \delta \\ u - \mu \tilde{\Pi} \end{bmatrix}, \quad \mathbf{G}(\mathbf{x}) = \begin{bmatrix} \sigma_\delta \Vert\delta\Vert \mathbf{I}_d & \mathbf{0}_{d \times 1} \\ \mathbf{0}_{1 \times d} & \sigma_\Pi \tilde{\Pi} \end{bmatrix}

The diffusion covariance matrix $\mathbf{D}(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{G}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{G}(\mathbf{x})^T$ is:

\mathbf{D}(\mathbf{x}) = \begin{bmatrix} \sigma_\delta^2 \Vert\delta\Vert^2 \mathbf{I}_d & \mathbf{0} \\ \mathbf{0} & \sigma_\Pi^2 \tilde{\Pi}^2 \end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{(d+1) \times (d+1)}

Metabolic Cost Functional

The objective of the active inference controller is to minimize expected cumulative homeostatic discrepancy and metabolic costs over time horizon $[t, T]$:

J(t, \mathbf{x}, u) = \mathbb{E} \left[ \int_t^T L(\mathbf{x}(s), u(s)) \, ds + \Phi(\mathbf{x}(T)) \;\middle\vert\; \mathbf{x}(t) = \mathbf{x} \right]

with running cost function $L(\mathbf{x}, u)$:

L(\mathbf{x}, u) = \underbrace{\frac{1}{2} \Vert\delta\Vert^2}_{\text{Homeostatic Discrepancy Cost}} + \underbrace{\gamma_1 \tilde{\Pi}}_{\text{Basal Precision Maintenance Cost}} + \underbrace{\frac{\gamma_2}{2} u^2}_{\text{Active Control Effort Metric}}

where $\gamma_1, \gamma_2 > 0$ parameterize basal metabolic tax and dynamic precision control costs, respectively.

7.2 Dynamic Programming Principle and Itô Expansion

Define the Value Function $V(\mathbf{x}, t)$ as the minimal achievable expected cost from state $\mathbf{x}$ at time $t$:

V(\mathbf{x}, t) = \inf_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \mathbb{E} \left[ \int_t^T L(\mathbf{x}(s), u(s)) \, ds + \Phi(\mathbf{x}(T)) \;\middle\vert\; \mathbf{x}(t) = \mathbf{x} \right]

Applying Bellman's Dynamic Programming Principle (DPP) over an infinitesimal step $\Delta t > 0$:

V(\mathbf{x}, t) = \inf_{u \in \mathcal{U}_{[t, t+\Delta t]}} \mathbb{E}_t \left[ \int_t^{t+\Delta t} L(\mathbf{x}(s), u(s)) \, ds + V(\mathbf{x}(t+\Delta t), t+\Delta t) \right]

Expanding $V(\mathbf{x}(t+\Delta t), t+\Delta t)$ around $(\mathbf{x}, t)$ using multidimensional Itô's Lemma gives:

V(\mathbf{x}(t+\Delta t), t+\Delta t) = V(\mathbf{x}, t) + \frac{\partial V}{\partial t} \Delta t + (\nabla_\mathbf{x} V)^T d\mathbf{x} + \frac{1}{2} \text{Tr}\left( \mathbf{D}(\mathbf{x}) \nabla_{\mathbf{x}}^2 V \right) \Delta t + o(\Delta t)

Taking the conditional expectation $\mathbb{E}_t [\cdot]$ and substituting $d\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}, u) \Delta t + \mathbf{G}(\mathbf{x}) d\mathbf{W}$:

\mathbb{E}_t \left[ V(\mathbf{x}(t+\Delta t), t+\Delta t) \right] = V(\mathbf{x}, t) + \left[ \frac{\partial V}{\partial t} + \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}, u)^T \nabla_\mathbf{x} V + \frac{1}{2} \text{Tr}\left( \mathbf{D}(\mathbf{x}) \nabla_{\mathbf{x}}^2 V \right) \right] \Delta t + o(\Delta t)

Approximating the integral term as $\mathbb{E}_t \left[ \int_t^{t+\Delta t} L(\mathbf{x}(s), u(s)) ds \right] = L(\mathbf{x}, u) \Delta t + o(\Delta t)$, we substitute back into the DPP:

V(\mathbf{x}, t) = \inf_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \left\{ L(\mathbf{x}, u) \Delta t + V(\mathbf{x}, t) + \left[ \frac{\partial V}{\partial t} + \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}, u)^T \nabla_\mathbf{x} V + \frac{1}{2} \text{Tr}\left( \mathbf{D}(\mathbf{x}) \nabla_{\mathbf{x}}^2 V \right) \right] \Delta t + o(\Delta t) \right\}

Subtracting $V(\mathbf{x}, t)$ from both sides, dividing by $\Delta t$, and taking the limit $\Delta t \to 0^+$ yields the general HJB equation:

-\frac{\partial V}{\partial t} = \inf_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \left\{ L(\mathbf{x}, u) + \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}, u)^T \nabla_\mathbf{x} V + \frac{1}{2} \text{Tr}\left( \mathbf{D}(\mathbf{x}) \nabla_{\mathbf{x}}^2 V \right) \right\}

7.3 Explicit Hamiltonian Minimization

Define the spatial gradient and Hessian matrix components:

\nabla_\mathbf{x} V = \begin{bmatrix} \nabla_\delta V \\ \frac{\partial V}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}} \end{bmatrix}, \quad \nabla_{\mathbf{x}}^2 V = \begin{bmatrix} \nabla_\delta^2 V & \frac{\partial \nabla_\delta V}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}} \\ \left(\frac{\partial \nabla_\delta V}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}}\right)^T & \frac{\partial^2 V}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}^2} \end{bmatrix}

Expanding the drift and diffusion trace operator terms:

\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}, u)^T \nabla_\mathbf{x} V = -k_r (\Pi_0 + \tilde{\Pi}) \delta^T \nabla_\delta V + (u - \mu \tilde{\Pi}) \frac{\partial V}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}}

\frac{1}{2} \text{Tr}\left( \mathbf{D}(\mathbf{x}) \nabla_{\mathbf{x}}^2 V \right) = \frac{1}{2} \sigma_\delta^2 \Vert\delta\Vert^2 \Delta_\delta V + \frac{1}{2} \sigma_\Pi^2 \tilde{\Pi}^2 \frac{\partial^2 V}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}^2}

where $\Delta_\delta V = \sum_{i=1}^d \frac{\partial^2 V}{\partial \delta_i^2}$ is the spatial Laplacian with respect to interoceptive innovation $\delta$.

The stochastic Hamiltonian $\mathcal{H}\left(\mathbf{x}, u, \nabla_\mathbf{x} V, \nabla_\mathbf{x}^2 V\right)$ becomes:

\mathcal{H} = \frac{1}{2} \Vert\delta\Vert^2 + \gamma_1 \tilde{\Pi} + \frac{\gamma_2}{2} u^2 - k_r (\Pi_0 + \tilde{\Pi}) \delta^T \nabla_\delta V + (u - \mu \tilde{\Pi}) \frac{\partial V}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}} + \frac{1}{2} \sigma_\delta^2 \Vert\delta\Vert^2 \Delta_\delta V + \frac{1}{2} \sigma_\Pi^2 \tilde{\Pi}^2 \frac{\partial^2 V}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}^2}

To find the optimal control policy $u^*$, differentiate $\mathcal{H}$ with respect to $u$ and set the derivative to zero:

\frac{\partial \mathcal{H}}{\partial u} = \gamma_2 u + \frac{\partial V}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}} = 0 \implies u^*(\mathbf{x}, t) = -\frac{1}{\gamma_2} \frac{\partial V}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}}

Physical Interpretation: Optimal precision driving effort $u^*$ is directly proportional to the marginal reduction in future interoceptive cost with respect to increased precision (i.e., $-\frac{\partial V}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}}$), inversely scaled by control metabolic effort cost $\gamma_2$.

Substituting $u^*(\mathbf{x}, t)$ back into the Hamiltonian:

\frac{\gamma_2}{2} (u^*)^2 + u^* \frac{\partial V}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}} = \frac{\gamma_2}{2} \left( -\frac{1}{\gamma_2} \frac{\partial V}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}} \right)^2 - \frac{1}{\gamma_2} \left( \frac{\partial V}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}} \right)^2 = -\frac{1}{2 \gamma_2} \left( \frac{\partial V}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}} \right)^2

7.4 Non-linear HJB Partial Differential Equation

Combining the optimized Hamiltonian terms gives the full non-linear second-order stochastic HJB PDE for interoceptive precision modulation:

-\frac{\partial V}{\partial t} = \frac{1}{2} \Vert\delta\Vert^2 + \gamma_1 \tilde{\Pi} - k_r (\Pi_0 + \tilde{\Pi}) \delta^T \nabla_\delta V - \mu \tilde{\Pi} \frac{\partial V}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}} - \frac{1}{2 \gamma_2} \left( \frac{\partial V}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}} \right)^2 + \frac{1}{2} \sigma_\delta^2 \Vert\delta\Vert^2 \Delta_\delta V + \frac{1}{2} \sigma_\Pi^2 \tilde{\Pi}^2 \frac{\partial^2 V}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}^2}

with terminal boundary condition at horizon $T$:

V(\delta, \tilde{\Pi}, T) = \Phi(\delta, \tilde{\Pi})

7.5 Infinite-Horizon Stationary Homeostatic Formulation

For stationary organismic homeostatic regulation ($T \to \infty$) with metabolic discount rate $\rho > 0$, the value function separates as $V(\mathbf{x}, t) = e^{-\rho t} v(\mathbf{x})$. The stationary HJB PDE reduces to:

\rho v(\delta, \tilde{\Pi}) = \frac{1}{2} \Vert\delta\Vert^2 + \gamma_1 \tilde{\Pi} - k_r (\Pi_0 + \tilde{\Pi}) \delta^T \nabla_\delta v - \mu \tilde{\Pi} \frac{\partial v}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}} - \frac{1}{2 \gamma_2} \left( \frac{\partial v}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}} \right)^2 + \frac{1}{2} \sigma_\delta^2 \Vert\delta\Vert^2 \Delta_\delta v + \frac{1}{2} \sigma_\Pi^2 \tilde{\Pi}^2 \frac{\partial^2 v}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}^2}

Summary of Term Structures

Term in HJB Equation Mathematical Expression Biological/Control Significance
Discounted Cost State $\rho v(\delta, \tilde{\Pi})$ Total expected stationary interoceptive cost
Homeostatic Risk $\frac{1}{2} \Vert\delta\Vert^2$ Immediate metabolic penalization of prediction errors
Basal Tax $\gamma_1 \tilde{\Pi}$ Baseline physiological cost of maintaining elevated precision
Error Decay Drift $-k_r (\Pi_0 + \tilde{\Pi}) \delta^T \nabla_\delta v$ Cost reduction via precision-weighted error dissipation
Metabolic Control Gain $-\frac{1}{2\gamma_2} \left(\frac{\partial v}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}}\right)^2$ Optimal active metabolic effort driving precision shifts
State Noise Diffusion $\frac{1}{2}\sigma_\delta^2 \Vert\delta\Vert^2 \Delta_\delta v$ Variance penalty from sensory innovation fluctuation
Precision Diffusion $\frac{1}{2}\sigma_\Pi^2 \tilde{\Pi}^2 \frac{\partial^2 v}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}^2}$ Stochastic uncertainty in neuromodulatory precision gain

7.6 Local Quadratic Approximation and LQR Solution

To approximate the non-linear HJB PDE around the deterministic homeostatic equilibrium $(\delta^*, \tilde{\Pi}^*) = (\mathbf{0}, 0)$, we perform a local second-order Taylor expansion of the value function $v(\delta, \tilde{\Pi})$:

v(\delta, \tilde{\Pi}) \approx v_0 + \frac{1}{2} \delta^T \mathbf{P}_{\delta\delta} \delta + p_{\delta\Pi} \delta^T \mathbf{1}_d \, \tilde{\Pi} + \frac{1}{2} P_{\Pi\Pi} \tilde{\Pi}^2

By symmetry and isotropy around equilibrium, cross-directional terms cancel, yielding $\mathbf{P}_{\delta\delta} = P_{\delta\delta} \mathbf{I}_d$ and $p_{\delta\Pi} = 0$. Thus, the local value function simplifies to:

v(\delta, \tilde{\Pi}) \approx v_0 + \frac{1}{2} P_{\delta\delta} \Vert\delta\Vert^2 + \frac{1}{2} P_{\Pi\Pi} \tilde{\Pi}^2

where $P_{\delta\delta} > 0$ and $P_{\Pi\Pi} > 0$ are the unknown positive-definite scalar coefficients representing local curvature along state error and precision dimensions.

Partial Derivatives and Linearized Terms

Evaluating the required spatial partial derivatives under this quadratic ansatz:

\nabla_\delta v = P_{\delta\delta} \delta, \quad \Delta_\delta v = \sum_{i=1}^d \frac{\partial^2 v}{\partial \delta_i^2} = d \cdot P_{\delta\delta}

\frac{\partial v}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}} = P_{\Pi\Pi} \tilde{\Pi}, \quad \frac{\partial^2 v}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}^2} = P_{\Pi\Pi}

Substituting these partial derivatives into the stationary HJB PDE:

\rho \left( v_0 + \frac{1}{2} P_{\delta\delta} \Vert\delta\Vert^2 + \frac{1}{2} P_{\Pi\Pi} \tilde{\Pi}^2 \right) = \frac{1}{2} \Vert\delta\Vert^2 + \gamma_1 \tilde{\Pi} - k_r (\Pi_0 + \tilde{\Pi}) P_{\delta\delta} \Vert\delta\Vert^2 - \mu P_{\Pi\Pi} \tilde{\Pi}^2 - \frac{P_{\Pi\Pi}^2}{2\gamma_2} \tilde{\Pi}^2 + \frac{1}{2} \sigma_\delta^2 \Vert\delta\Vert^2 (d \cdot P_{\delta\delta}) + \frac{1}{2} \sigma_\Pi^2 P_{\Pi\Pi} \tilde{\Pi}^2

Matching Coefficients Order by Order

Order $\mathcal{O}(\Vert\delta\Vert^2)$ Equation: State Error Curvature

Gathering all terms proportional to $\Vert\delta\Vert^2$:

\frac{1}{2} \rho P_{\delta\delta} = \frac{1}{2} - k_r \Pi_0 P_{\delta\delta} + \frac{1}{2} d \, \sigma_\delta^2 P_{\delta\delta}

Grouping terms with $P_{\delta\delta}$:

P_{\delta\delta} \left[ \frac{\rho}{2} + k_r \Pi_0 - \frac{1}{2} d \, \sigma_\delta^2 \right] = \frac{1}{2}

Solving explicitly for $P_{\delta\delta}$:

P_{\delta\delta} = \frac{1}{\rho + 2 k_r \Pi_0 - d \, \sigma_\delta^2}

Existence Condition: For $P_{\delta\delta} > 0$ (a well-defined value function), we require:

\rho + 2 k_r \Pi_0 > d \, \sigma_\delta^2

This requires the baseline interoceptive restoring force ($2 k_r \Pi_0$) plus metabolic discounting ($\rho$) to strictly dominate total state spatial diffusion variance ($d \, \sigma_\delta^2$).

Order $\mathcal{O}(\tilde{\Pi}^2)$ Equation: Algebraic Riccati Equation for Precision

Gathering all terms quadratic in precision shift $\tilde{\Pi}^2$:

\frac{1}{2} \rho P_{\Pi\Pi} = -\mu P_{\Pi\Pi} - \frac{1}{2 \gamma_2} P_{\Pi\Pi}^2 + \frac{1}{2} \sigma_\Pi^2 P_{\Pi\Pi}

Assuming $P_{\Pi\Pi} \neq 0$ and dividing through by $P_{\Pi\Pi}$:

\frac{1}{2} \rho = -\mu - \frac{1}{2 \gamma_2} P_{\Pi\Pi} + \frac{1}{2} \sigma_\Pi^2

Rearranging into standard algebraic form:

\frac{1}{2 \gamma_2} P_{\Pi\Pi} = \frac{1}{2} \sigma_\Pi^2 - \mu - \frac{1}{2} \rho

P_{\Pi\Pi} = \gamma_2 \left( \sigma_\Pi^2 - 2\mu - \rho \right)

For $P_{\Pi\Pi} > 0$, stochastic precision volatility must strictly exceed baseline precision damping and discounting ($\sigma_\Pi^2 > 2\mu + \rho$).

When cross-coupling drive $\kappa \cdot \sigma_\eta(\Vert\delta\Vert^2) (\Delta\Pi_{\max} - \tilde{\Pi})$ is linearized around $\Vert\delta\Vert \approx 0$, drive expands as $\frac{2 \kappa \Delta\Pi_{\max}}{\eta} \Vert\delta\Vert^2$. Including this third-order cross-coupling modifies the algebraic Riccati equation for precision cost $P_{\Pi\Pi}$ to:

\frac{1}{2 \gamma_2} P_{\Pi\Pi}^2 + (\rho + 2\mu - \sigma_\Pi^2) P_{\Pi\Pi} - Q_{\Pi\Pi} = 0

where $Q_{\Pi\Pi} > 0$ represents state-driven precision demand. Solving this quadratic Riccati equation for the unique positive root $P_{\Pi\Pi}^*$:

P_{\Pi\Pi}^* = \gamma_2 \left( \sqrt{(\rho + 2\mu - \sigma_\Pi^2)^2 + \frac{2 Q_{\Pi\Pi}}{\gamma_2}} - (\rho + 2\mu - \sigma_\Pi^2) \right)

Order $\mathcal{O}(1)$ Equation: Constant Value $v_0$

Matching the residual constant terms at equilibrium yields the baseline stationary homeostatic cost $v_0$:

\rho v_0 = 0 \implies v_0 = 0

Local Linear-Quadratic Regulator (LQR) Optimal Gain

Recalling the exact continuous feedback law derived from the HJB Hamiltonian:

u^*(\delta, \tilde{\Pi}) = -\frac{1}{\gamma_2} \frac{\partial v}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}}

Substituting the quadratic local derivative $\frac{\partial v}{\partial \tilde{\Pi}} \approx P_{\Pi\Pi}^* \tilde{\Pi}$:

u^*(\tilde{\Pi}) \approx -K_{\Pi} \tilde{\Pi}

where the scalar optimal LQR gain $K_{\Pi}$ is given by:

K_{\Pi} = \frac{P_{\Pi\Pi}^*}{\gamma_2} = \sqrt{(\rho + 2\mu - \sigma_\Pi^2)^2 + \frac{2 Q_{\Pi\Pi}}{\gamma_2}} - (\rho + 2\mu - \sigma_\Pi^2)

Summary of Closed-Form LQR Solutions

\begin{aligned}
P_{\delta\delta}^* &= \frac{1}{\rho + 2 k_r \Pi_0 - d \, \sigma_\delta^2} \\
P_{\Pi\Pi}^* &= \gamma_2 \left( \sqrt{(\rho + 2\mu - \sigma_\Pi^2)^2 + \frac{2 Q_{\Pi\Pi}}{\gamma_2}} - (\rho + 2\mu - \sigma_\Pi^2) \right) \\
K_{\Pi} &= \frac{P_{\Pi\Pi}^*}{\gamma_2}
\end{aligned}

This LQR solution provides a normative grounding for the precision modulation architecture: the optimal control policy is a linear feedback law that drives precision toward its homeostatic baseline, with the gain determined by the trade-off between metabolic control costs and the value of reducing interoceptive prediction error. The saturated precision dynamics of Chapter 5 can be understood as a constrained implementation of this optimal policy, where the saturation $\Pi_{\max}$ prevents the gain from becoming unbounded.

